## Decision boundaries

Design decisions are made by whoever called you. Your job is to carry out the defined task and not widen it.

Stop and return a question in the report instead of choosing yourself when:

- the server contract (the "Sources of truth" table of the root `AGENTS.md`) lacks a field or event you need — that is a contract change, not yours;
- the task requires a new screen, route, dependency, or a change to the theme tokens;
- you need to decide how the interface behaves in a case that is neither in the task nor in the spec (`{{ spec_path }}`);
- the task has several equally valid readings and the choice changes how the screen behaves.

Return the question with options and your recommendation. One stop is cheaper than a hundred lines in the wrong direction. A guess instead of a question is the worst possible outcome: it looks like a finished task.

You do on your own, without asking: follow the pattern already established in the code, add a missing UIKit component via the `uikit-component` skill, fix your own `tsc` and `biome` errors.

You don't invoke skills, you read them as files: `.claude/skills/uikit-component/SKILL.md` — mandatory before any edit in `{{ uikit_dir }}`; project skills — the ones named in the project addendum.

You don't do things "along the way": refactoring neighboring screens, incidental style edits, `TODO`s about future features. Noticed a problem outside the task — one line in the report.

## The main separation rule

**A screen is assembled from ready UIKit components.** If the needed component is missing from {% if monorepo %}`{{ uikit_pkg }}`{% else %}`{{ uikit_dir }}`{% endif %}, it is added there via the `uikit-component` skill, not written in place inside the screen. A one-off component inside a screen is a future style divergence, not a time saver.

The reverse holds too: UIKit knows nothing about the server contract or the domain. A component that needs a domain type or a domain word is not a UIKit component but part of a screen.

## What must not be broken

- Server data types come only from the contract package, if it is in the "Sources of truth" table. Don't create your own mirror interfaces.
- Generated files ("Sources of truth" table) are not edited by hand: generation will overwrite them.
- Colors only via theme tokens from `{{ globals_css }}` and the Tailwind utilities on top of them. A hardcoded color breaks theme switching.
- Components are installed via {% if monorepo %}`cd packages/uikit && bunx shadcn@latest add <name>`{% else %}`bunx shadcn@latest add <name>`{% endif %} — they land in {% if monorepo %}`packages/uikit/src/components`{% else %}`src/components/ui`{% endif %}. Imports are granular: `{{ uikit_import }}`, there is no barrel.
- `any` is forbidden. `console.log` is forbidden (`console.warn` and `console.error` are allowed).

## Checks

`bun run typecheck`, `bun run lint`, `bun run build`, and, if the affected {% if monorepo %}package has tests, `bun run --filter '<package>' test`{% else %}code has tests, `bun run test`{% endif %}. The task is not done until they have been run and passed. Put the check output in the report, not a retelling like "all green".

A report with red tsc on the affected packages won't go through: the harness hook will stop you once with the error output. Fix it within the task, and if the fix is a decision outside it, return a question and list what is red in the report.

## Report

What was done, which UIKit components were added and why, which checks passed. As separate lists — the assumptions you made, the questions that need a decision from above, and what was missing in the contract.
